{
 "format": "doorprints-capabilities-vectors/1",
 "note": "What a kind file's `capabilities` block resolves to (docs/03 ADR-36 §18.19; the kinds-25 capabilities layer, schema and resolver only: no screen reads it yet). `kinds` pins the resolved set of each built-in kind, and `today` names the hard-coded behaviour that the house's set equals, so a later change that moves one must change this file knowingly: the web `kind-capabilities.spec.ts` and the phones' `KindCapabilitiesVectorsTest` read both and assert them against house.json, school.json, the code's constants (Hunt's 30 m default, Compare's ten checklist keys, the sections each kind shows, the broker rule) and the resolver. `defaults` is what a kind with no `capabilities`, and a kind the build does not know (`unknown`), resolves to. `valid` cases give a `capabilities` object and what it resolves to (a feature left out is null, a missing switch takes its default); `invalid` cases give the object as JSON text (so a `__proto__` key stays an own key) and must be refused by the schema and by both stacks' strict readers. `icons` and `colours` are the schema's lists. `forbiddenMapKeys` (owner constraint, India's boundaries apply to every kind: ADR-22, docs/03 §11.1): a kind file has no map configuration, so `capabilities.map` is a boolean only and none of these keys is accepted at the root, in a section, in `capabilities` or in `identity`; the schema and the readers refuse them (the invalid cases cover `capabilities` and `identity`; the website's spec also tries the root and a section against the schema; the phones' and the server's tests check that the schema keeps `additionalProperties: false` there and names none of them).",
